  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_20a_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "In total, 22 articles [16 - 18, 24 - 28, 34 - 47] were included (Fig. 1). The number of people (with diabetes and COVID-19) ranged from 29 (smallest study) to 9460 (largest study). In total, our systematic review included 17,687 individuals. ... Most of the studies ( n = 14) were conducted in Asia (China, n = 8; South Korea, n = 3; Singapore, n = 1; Iran, n = 1; Israel n = 1), whilst five studies were conducted in North America (USA, n = 4; Mexico, n = 1) and three studies in Europe (France, n = 1; Fig. 1 Flow chart of the literature search Italy, n = 1; Spain, n = 1). The majority of the studies were conducted in the hospital setting and used data from hospital-based records, with a few exceptions: one study included data from a national registry [17] and two used data from health insurance records [40, 41]. Type of diabetes was not specified in n = 13 studies, n = 5 studies only included individuals with type 2 diabetes and n = 4 studies focused on both type 1 and type 2 diabetes. The characteristics of the studies are shown in Table 1 and more detailed information about the setting, the identified exposures and considered confounders in each study is shown in ESM Table 5. Risk of bias was low in n = 6 studies, moderate in n = 8 studies and high in n = 8 studies (ESM Fig. 1). The main reason for high risk of bias was insufficient adjustment for confounding factors and/or inappropriate statistical analysis and reporting of the findings (ESM Fig. 2).",
      "reason": "The Results section provides a brief summary of study characteristics (number of studies, total participants, study locations, settings, diabetes type distribution) and reports counts of studies by risk-of-bias level along with reasons for high risk."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_20a_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "There is high certainty of evidence that male sex compared with female sex was associated with increased risk of COVID19-related death (SRR 1.28 [95% CI 1.02, 1.61]; n = 10 studies) and COVID-19 severity (SRR 1.36 [95% CI 1.13, 1.64]; n = 11 studies) in individuals with diabetes and COVID-19. In addition, older age (>65 years) was associated with higher risk of COVID-19-related death (SRR 3.49 [95% CI 1.82, 6.69]; n = 6 studies; moderate certainty of evidence) and with COVID-19 severity (SRR 1.67 [95% CI 1.00; 2.76]; n = 6 studies; low certainty of evidence). ... With each 5 year increase in age, the relative risk for COVID-19-related death increased by 43% (95% CI 12%, 83%; n = 5 studies) and for severity by 25% (95% CI 11%, 40%; n = 7 studies), both with moderate certainty of evidence.",
